< Two players meet again and again, in the game called the prisoner's dilemma. Each time, each can cooperate or cheat (from here on, defect), and cheating pays unless both do it. Now and then a move comes out wrong; I call that a slip. It is a small picture of a common bind: both sides do well if both cooperate, and each is tempted to cheat. Which ways of playing survive? I let a hundred small machines, each one a way of playing, breed and mutate to find out, and when a few moves in a hundred slipped, the population kept coming back to the same machine. I called it the handshake and thought I had found something. Others had found it in 2006, also by evolving machines, and in 2012 it was analysed more thoroughly than I managed.
---
> Two players meet again and again, in the game called the prisoner's dilemma. Each time, each can cooperate or cheat (from here on, defect), and cheating pays unless both do it. Now and then a move comes out wrong; I call that a slip. It is a small picture of a common bind: both sides do well if both cooperate, and each is tempted to cheat. Which ways of playing survive? I let a hundred small machines, each one a way of playing, breed and mutate to find out, and when a few moves in a hundred slipped, the population kept coming back to the same machine: it defects until the other side has defected twice in a row, and then cooperates for as long as they do. I called it the handshake and thought I had found something. Others had found it in 2006, also by evolving machines, and in 2012 it was analysed more thoroughly than I managed.
