>  Iwasaki, Joe, Kandori, Obara and Yokoo (2012) have the same three states, begun at the cooperating one, under the name two-period mutual punishment. They went through every machine with up to three states, asked of each whether any possible way of playing could gain against it when each player can misread the other's move, and singled this one out, for misreadings that each player makes on its own. Where both players misread together, they found that it fails and tit-for-tat (cooperate first, then do whatever the other player did last) does best.
